FINDER(1)                      P32W Unix                       FINDER(1)

NAME
       finder - a directory's files as pictures: open, rename, move

SYNOPSIS
       finder [directory]

DESCRIPTION
       Under the window manager (wm(1)), finder shows the files
       of a directory (the one it is run in, or the one named) in
       a window, each as a small picture with its name under it:

       a folder             a directory
       a window             a program
       a framed picture     a picture (a PBM file, as view(1)
                            shows and wm's Snapshot writes)
       a page with lines    text
       a page with a mark   anything else

       The files whose names begin with a dot are not shown, as
       ls(1) leaves them out; .. is, the directory above.

       The top line has the words File and Apps, which are menus,
       and the directory's name. The last line tells how many
       files there are, or, when one is picked, its name and its
       particulars as ls -l has them; what could not be done is
       said there too. A name too long for its place is cut
       short and ends in ~ . The window's corner makes it bigger
       or smaller, a file's place at a time: as many files show
       as there is room for.

       It is P8X's finder brought to this machine. There the
       finder is the whole screen and becomes the program it
       starts; here it is one window among the others, and what
       it starts has windows of its own and runs beside it.

THE MOUSE
       on a file           picks it. On the one that is picked
                           already, opens it
       on File or Apps     lets the menu down while the button is
                           held; the item it is let go on is
                           chosen
       on the bar at the right
                           on its upper half, a page back; on its
                           lower half, a page on. The box in it is
                           as much of the files as shows

       Opening a directory goes into it (.. is the one above). A
       program is started, and any other file is shown by view(1):
       each in a window of its own, as win(1) starts them, in the
       directory shown.

THE FILE MENU
       Its items are for the file that is picked; each has a key
       that does the same.

       Open                as a press on the picked file
       Edit                the file in vi(1), in a window of 80
                           by 24
       Rename        R     another name for it, in this directory
       Duplicate     D     a copy of it under another name (a
                           file, not a directory)
       Move          M     it goes into the directory named, under
                           its own name. To another disk a file is
                           copied and then removed here
       New folder    N     a directory is made here
       Remove        X     the file is removed, or the directory
                           if it is empty, after y is typed

       A name is asked for on the last line: what is typed shows
       there, Backspace takes a character back, Return ends it,
       and ESC or a press of the mouse gives it up. Nothing takes
       the place of a file that is there already: it is said, and
       nothing is done.

THE APPS MENU
       The programs of /etc/apps, to be started: a line for each
       (eight at most), its name, a colon, and the command that
       starts it, which the shell runs in the directory shown.
       A command that begins with win has a window of its own. A
       line that begins with # is passed over.

              Clock:win clock
              Terminal:win -t -s 64x16 sh

KEYS
       With its window in front:

       h, j, k, l     pick the file to the left, below, above, to
                      the right (the arrows do the same)
       Return         open the one that is picked
       e              edit it
       R, D, M, N, X  the File menu's items
       u, Backspace   go to the directory above
       space, b       a page on, a page back
       r              read the directory again
       q              end

EXAMPLES
       win finder /usr/examples
       finder
              from a terminal's shell: the terminal waits until
              it ends
       wm -s /usr/examples/finder.hand
              a hand plays it: files renamed, copied, moved and
              removed, a folder made, the clock started

       It is also the Desk menu's Finder.

FILES
       /etc/apps                  the Apps menu
       /usr/src/cmd/finder.c      its source

SEE ALSO
       wm(1), win(1), files(1), view(1), mv(1), cp(1), rm(1),
       mkdir(1), gfx(3)

DIAGNOSTICS
       finder: not in a window of wm, when it is run anywhere
       else. What could not be done, and why, is said on the last
       line.

BUGS
       The first 512 files of a directory are shown.
       The mouse's other buttons do nothing: wm tells a program
       of the left one only.
       What a program that was started writes, or why it could
       not be started, is in its window, which goes when it ends.
